Let’s use click package in Python to parse command line arguments
Create a new python script called otter_greeting.py:
import click

@click.command()
@click.argument('count', type=int)
@click.argument('name', type=str)
def hello(count, name):
    """Simple program that greets NAME for a total of COUNT times."""
    for x in range(count):
        click.echo(f"🦦: `Nice to meet you, {name}!`")

if __name__ == '__main__':
    hello()

Let’s use click package in Python to parse command line arguments
Let’s use options instead of arguments to parse command line arguments:
import click

@click.command()
@click.option('--count', type=int, default=1, help='Number of greetings.')
@click.option('--name', type=str, prompt='Your name', help='The person to greet.')
def hello(count, name):
    """Simple program that greets NAME for a total of COUNT times."""
    for x in range(count):
        click.echo(f"🦦: `Nice to meet you, {name}!`")

if __name__ == '__main__':
    hello()

How many packages did you actually get?
The breast cancer predictor’s pyproject.toml vs its uv.lock:

viewof lock_pick = Inputs.select(lock_asked, {label: "Pick a package you asked for:", value: "pandas"})







html`&lt;div class="lock-head"&gt;&lt;span class="asked"&gt;${lock_summary.asked} asked for&lt;/span&gt; in pyproject.toml, &lt;span class="pinned"&gt;${lock_summary.pinned} pinned&lt;/span&gt; in uv.lock&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="ojs-readout" style="text-align: center;"&gt;${Object.assign(document.createElement("span"), {innerHTML: lock_readout[lock_pick]})}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="lock-grid"&gt;${lock_pinned.map(name =&gt; {
  const kind = [
    "lock-chip",
    lock_asked.includes(name) ? "asked" : "",
    name === lock_pick ? "pick" : "",
    lock_pulls[lock_pick].includes(name) ? "on" : "",
    name in lock_os_only ? "os" : ""
  ].join(" ");
  const tag = name in lock_os_only ? html`&lt;small&gt; ${lock_os_only[name]} only&lt;/small&gt;` : "";
  return html`&lt;span class="${kind}"&gt;${name}${tag}&lt;/span&gt;`;
})}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="lock-key"&gt;&lt;span class="c-blue"&gt;blue&lt;/span&gt;: in pyproject.toml, &lt;span class="c-orange"&gt;orange&lt;/span&gt;: pulled in by your pick, dashed: only installed on one operating system&lt;/div&gt;`








Start on pandas: it pulls in only a few packages.
Pick altair, then ipykernel: ipykernel pulls in the most (it brings the whole Jupyter machinery).
Point at the dashed chips: appnope is only installed on macOS, colorama only on Windows. One uv.lock holds the answer for every operating system.

Q: if your pyproject.toml lists only 11 packages, who decides the versions of the other ones? Answer: without a lock file, whatever is newest on the day you install. With uv.lock, everyone gets the same ones.

